  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper low will remain centered just to the east \nheading into tomorrow, keeping the region in a seasonably cool \nairmass. Skies will continue to clear as it inches to the east and \nby tonight, the clouds should be mostly gone. This will set the \nstage for efficient radiational cooling, with lows generally falling \ninto the 40s and upper 30s. The current forecast low at SLK is 37, \nbut it could easily fall a few more degrees if winds go calm early. \nWarm air advection will begin for Monday and Tuesday, but it will be \nunsuccessful in causing a significant airmass change, with the \nhumidity staying low and temperatures remaining around seasonable \nvalues. A few showers are possible in northern areas Monday night \nand early Tuesday morning as a dying cold front passes into the \nregion.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A very humid air mass will quickly advect into Vermont \nand northern New York late Wednesday into early Thursday due to \nchanneled flow stretching across the Great Lakes into New England \nwith 100+ knot flow at 300mb. Flow will be fast down at 850mb as \nwell, though the intensity of the LLJ has backed off towards 40-45 \nknots. It will still result in a short duration of 2\" PWATs across \nthe region. Departing high pressure and the fast mid-level flow will \nresult in fairly strong isentropic upglide. There should also be \nmarginal CAPE of about 250 J/kg after dark on Wednesday for at least \nthe St. Lawrence Valley (LREF ~40% > 250 J/kg, but expect that to \npercentage to increase as we get into the mesoscale model window). \nSo moderate rain, perhaps briefly heavy, will race eastwards. Again, \nsynoptic models are likely broad brushing rainfall amounts too much \ngiven the LLJ will produce west slope orographic enhancement and \neast slope terrain shadowing. The residence time of this moisture \nwill be very short (5-9 hours) due to the fast flow, which will \nprevent significant rainfall totals. Broadly speaking, totals appear \nto range about 0.25-0.75\", and we'll get towards the end of \nmesoscale models tomorrow for some of those finer details.\n\nOn Thursday, the tropical air mass will be gone, but there's still \ngoing to be a little moisture lingering. Marginal diurnally driven \ninstability could produce a few showers Thursday afternoon, but \nit'll be generally warm in the 70s to near 80 while we await a cold \nfront that'll pass through late Thursday night or Friday morning. \nMedium range guidance has shifted towards drier solutions for \nFriday. So we'll spend most all our weekend without rain as broad \nhigh pressure builds across the area. Some rain showers could begin \napproaching towards next Sunday evening when broad surface higher \npressure will begin shifting offshore.",
